  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Own the streaming-output path. Retain lexicographic generation behavior, but replace per-line std::endl flushing with a bounded reusable byte buffer and batched output. Compare coherent C++ stream buffering approaches without mixing unsafe I/O modes; do not retain all factorial-sized output. Preserve exact bytes, including the bracket/space layout, duplicate handling, empty input behavior, and non-C-string characters. Guard the terminal permutation scan if needed without changing observable output. Deliver one independently correct candidate and measured evidence.",
        "B": "Own sequential permutation-engine research. Replace the terminal iterator-underflow-prone scan with a standards-safe lexicographic successor implementation, comparing a corrected direct loop and std::next_permutation under the original char ordering. Explore fusing fixed-record formatting with generation only when it improves whole-program time, using bounded output buffering to isolate generation cost. Cover empty, singleton, all-equal, repeated-character, and final-descending states; do not use factorial/ranking shortcuts that overflow or specialize inputs. Deliver a complete valid alternative and measurements.",
        "C": "Own parallel feasibility for the four ARM64 cores. Investigate a general duplicate-aware lexicographic-prefix decomposition whose tasks are contiguous in required output order. Any workers must feed a bounded ordered coordinator; concurrent unordered writes or storing complete subtree output are invalid. Account for task imbalance, queue deadlock, and serialized-output limits, and use no compiler or runtime configuration changes. Benchmark against the best sequential transport path and return a parallel candidate only if whole-program measurements show a reliable win; otherwise report that parallelism is not worthwhile.",
        "G": "Integrate empirically, not additively. Start from independently valid A, B, and C artifacts; measure each whole candidate against the valid 0.154053878 s baseline, then test only explicitly compatible combinations with the same correctness process. Recheck exact global lexical ordering and byte-for-byte formatting after every integration. Do not alter compiler, target, budgets, tests, or oracle, and retain the fastest stable fully valid candidate rather than merging nominal improvements or forcing a parallel version.",
        "analysis": "Only measured evidence is a correct four-case baseline at 0.154053878 s, with no profile or candidate deltas. The main hypothesis is output cost: every generated permutation is serialized and std::endl flushes every line. The source strips exactly six whitespace characters, sorts under its existing char comparisons, and emits each distinct lexicographic permutation in a fixed bracket-and-space line format; all of that must remain exact for every input. Its final descending-state suffix scan appears to decrement before begin, so safe candidates should eliminate that undefined behavior. Output ordering makes the four-core parallel option constrained, and every proposed gain requires independent measurement."
      },
